>:I think that g-<move> is equivalent to <ctrl>-<move> but no one uses
>:g-<move> if they've got <ctrl>-<move>. g-<move> isn't as nice. Not having
>:<crtl>-<move> (i.e. not fully supported) _is_ a problem! What is the
>:alternative to <ctrl>-<move> on the pc? (I.e. would <alt>-<move> work?)
>     I tried using both g-<move> and ctrl-move (by disabling the number_pad
>option) and both of them go right past side-paths.

The Atari ST port suffers from this as well -- I'd decided not to
'complain' as I thought it was a problem with the port, but sounds like
it might be lower.

Basically, it allows you to use the keypad (which I much prefer) to get
'move' and 'shift-move' (and g-move) but not 'control-move'.  If you
want to use control-move you have to use the (non-mnemonic) vi
character keys yuhjklnm.  Don't get me wrong, I'm a big fan of vi, but
I don't use those move-keys except in my .exrc.  More annoying, it
appears to get this effect NOT by analyzing the codes returned from the
keys, but by actually forcing it's (nh's) own keyboard map.  Reason I
say this is that with old 'hack', I could map my keypad to make
shift-move send control-move, by running a little keyboard remapping
program first.  With nethack this doesn't work -- I can remap the
keyboard, sure, but then nethack stomps it into what IT wants.

What I'd prefer to see is for nethack to use less forceful techniques,
so that I can remap my keyboard from outside.  Failing that, (yet
another) option switch which would allow me to swap the meanings of
shift-move and control-move.

